      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hi, I'm looking at our HACCP plan for our catering kitchen. Can you give me the specific temperature and time requirements for cooling down cooked poultry? I need to make sure we're preventing Clostridium perfringens growth correctly. thanks.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To prevent *Clostridium perfringens* in a catering kitchen HACCP plan, you must cool cooked poultry from 135°F (57°C) to 70°F (21°C) within 2 hours, and then from 70°F to 41°F (5°C) or colder within a subsequent 4-hour period. This means the total cooling time from the initial temperature to 41°F or below must not exceed 6 hours. Always ensure the cooling is achieved using a method that brings the center of the thickest part, such as a large slow cooker insert or roast, through these zones quickly to prevent bacterial multiplication.",
